{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-100609",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(x+12)}{2} & =4 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x+12) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x+12) & =8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+12)} & ={2}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+12)}=x+12.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+12 & ={2}^{8} \\\\ x+12 & =256 \\\\ x & =244.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
